[{"component_name":"Martial Focus","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Martial Focus
Barrage uses Martial Focus.
Unless a broader martial-sphere rule later replaces this wording:
you may hold one Martial Focus at a time;
some talents expend Martial Focus;
expending Martial Focus is not an Action;
you cannot expend Martial Focus if you do not currently hold it;
if a talent says you regain Martial Focus, you regain it only if it is currently absent. Base Sphere Ability","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":6242,"source_global_line_start":6235,"source_heading":"Martial Focus","source_local_line_end":47,"source_local_line_start":40,"structured_fields":{}}]

[{"component_name":"Batter","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Batter
A Batter is a Special Attack made with:
a melee weapon;
an unarmed strike;
a natural weapon;
a thrown weapon.
On a hit that deals damage, the target becomes Battered until the end of your next turn.
A Battered creature has no automatic penalty by itself.
Exertion talents exploit the opening created by Batter.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":9410,"source_global_line_start":9402,"source_heading":"Batter","source_local_line_end":47,"source_local_line_start":39,"structured_fields":{}}]

[{"component_name":"Dream Seed","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Dream Seed
A Dream Seed is a symbolic impression planted in one creature’s subconscious.
A Dream Seed is not a visible mark.
It does not provide tracking, exact-square knowledge, or automatic detection.
Unless a talent says otherwise:
a Dream Seed on an awake unwilling creature lasts until the start of your next turn;
a Dream Seed on a willing or sleeping creature lasts for 1 hour;
a creature may bear only one of your Dream Seeds at a time;
a Dream Seed allows you to use Seed talents on that creature;
a Dream Seed ends if a talent consumes it. A sleeping creature does not automatically wake when a Dream Seed is planted.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":22846,"source_global_line_start":22837,"source_heading":"Dream Seed","source_local_line_end":22,"source_local_line_start":13,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Drowsy Thought","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Drowsy Thought - Seed Talent
When you learn the Dreams sphere, you learn Drowsy Thought automatically.
When an awake unwilling creature fails its saving throw against your Dream Seed, it also becomes Drowsy until the start of your next turn.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":22893,"source_global_line_start":22891,"source_heading":"Drowsy Thought - Seed Talent","source_local_line_end":69,"source_local_line_start":67,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Dreamscape","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Dreamscape
A Dreamscape is a symbolic mental environment overlaying waking space or experienced during sleep.
A Dreamscape may resemble:
an endless staircase;
a moonlit lake;
a childhood courtyard;
a burning city;
a forest of eyes;
a palace of mirrors;
a battlefield under falling snow;
a giant library;
floating islands;
a star-filled void. A Dreamscape does not automatically reveal literal truth.
A creature’s appearance inside may reflect emotion, memory, fear, desire, regret, self-image, spiritual damage, or hidden bonds.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":22874,"source_global_line_start":22861,"source_heading":"Dreamscape","source_local_line_end":50,"source_local_line_start":37,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Restful Dream","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Restful Dream - Rite Talent
When you learn the Dreams sphere, you learn Restful Dream automatically.
Action Type: Ritual Procedure
Canonical Action Type: Ritual Procedure
Factory Routing: procedure_ledger_not_combat_action
Timing Detail: Ritual
Time: 10 minutes
Range: Touch
Target: One willing creature
Duration: Until the end of the target’s next short rest
Cost: 1 CP
Guide the target into a restorative dream during its next short rest.
After completing that rest, the target:
gains Temporary HP equal to 2 x Proficiency Bonus;
may repeat one saving throw against Frightened;
may end one ordinary nightmare, minor sleep disturbance, or minor mental-stress effect;
gains Advantage on one check made to recover from ordinary exhaustion, stress, or mental fatigue. A creature may benefit from Restful Dream only once per long rest.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":22930,"source_global_line_start":22914,"source_heading":"Restful Dream - Rite Talent","source_local_line_end":106,"source_local_line_start":90,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Ritual Procedure","canonical_action_type":"Ritual Procedure","cost":"1 CP","duration":"Until the end of the target’s next short rest","factory_routing":"procedure_ledger_not_combat_action","range":"Touch","target":"One willing creature","timing_detail":"Ritual"}}]

[{"component_name":"Ambidexterity","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Ambidexterity
When you make the extra attack of the Light property as a Bonus Action, you may expend Martial Focus to make that attack as part of the Attack action instead.
You may use Ambidexterity only once per turn.
Ambidexterity:
changes the action timing of the Light-property extra attack;
does not create an additional attack;
does not grant an additional Bonus Action;
does not move an extra attack granted only by the Dual Wielder feat;
does not stack with Nick to create another Light-property extra attack.
If Nick already moves your Light-property extra attack into the Attack action, you do not need to expend Martial Focus to do so.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":25231,"source_global_line_start":25222,"source_heading":"Ambidexterity","source_local_line_end":119,"source_local_line_start":110,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Dual Draw","exact_source_excerpt":"##### Dual Draw
Whenever you would equip or unequip one weapon as part of the Attack action or another rule, you may equip or unequip two one-handed weapons instead.
Equipping includes drawing or picking up a weapon. Unequipping includes sheathing, stowing, or dropping a weapon.
Dual Draw does not:
allow you to interact with unrelated objects;
reload a weapon;
activate a magic item;
exceed the number of hands you actually possess;
change whether a weapon is one-handed or Two-Handed.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":25218,"source_global_line_start":25210,"source_heading":"Dual Draw","source_local_line_end":106,"source_local_line_start":98,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Paired Rhythm","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Paired Rhythm
Once per turn, when you hit one or more creatures with both weapons from your Qualifying Pair during the same turn, regain Martial Focus.
The two hits may target the same creature or different creatures.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":25221,"source_global_line_start":25219,"source_heading":"Paired Rhythm","source_local_line_end":109,"source_local_line_start":107,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Twin-Weapon Training","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Twin-Weapon Training
When you learn the Dual Wielding sphere, gain Balanced Defense and Dual Draw.
##### Balanced Defense
While wielding a Qualifying Pair, gain a +1 bonus to AC.
For stacking purposes, treat this bonus as a Shield bonus:
you do not gain this bonus while wielding a Shield;
if another feature says its AC benefit does not stack with a Shield, it does not stack with Balanced Defense;
Balanced Defense does not count as wielding a Shield for any other purpose;
Balanced Defense does not grant Shield training or satisfy a prerequisite that requires a Shield.
An Unarmed Strike or natural weapon does not qualify unless a specific rule explicitly allows it.
##### Dual Draw
Whenever you would equip or unequip one weapon as part of the Attack action or another rule, you may equip or unequip two one-handed weapons instead.
Equipping includes drawing or picking up a weapon. Unequipping includes sheathing, stowing, or dropping a weapon.
Dual Draw does not:
allow you to interact with unrelated objects;
reload a weapon;
activate a magic item;
exceed the number of hands you actually possess;
change whether a weapon is one-handed or Two-Handed.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":25218,"source_global_line_start":25200,"source_heading":"Twin-Weapon Training","source_local_line_end":106,"source_local_line_start":88,"structured_fields":{}}]

[{"component_name":"Training and Tools","exact_source_excerpt":"### Training and Tools
When you learn this sphere, gain proficiency with one of the following:
herbalism kit;
Nature;
Survival;
Medicine;
land vehicles;
an appropriate artisan’s tool such as woodcarver’s tools, mason’s tools, or cartographer’s tools.
If you already have the chosen proficiency, choose one Mortal Realm Harvesting and Gathering talent for which you meet the prerequisites as a bonus talent.
You also gain a basic gatherer’s kit if starting equipment is being assigned by class or background. A gatherer’s kit includes a small sickle, pruning knife, digging hook, cord, waxed paper, cloth wraps, small jars, simple gloves, and a field ledger.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":38439,"source_global_line_start":38430,"source_heading":"Training and Tools","source_local_line_end":79,"source_local_line_start":70,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Read the Sign","exact_source_excerpt":"### Read the Sign
Action Type: Multi-Mode
Canonical Action Type: Multi-Mode
Factory Combat Bucket: action
Timing Detail: Search or Study action, or 10 minutes of field examination
Factory Routing: combat_action_plus_field_examination_variant
Split Review Decision: single_combat_action_plus_procedure_variant
Recommended Factory Realization: procedures=TEN_MINUTE_FIELD_EXAMINATION
Range: 60 feet for obvious signs; 1 mile during wilderness survey; special talents may extend this
You examine plants, soil, tracks, insects, wind, dew, beast leavings, mineral seams, spirit pressure, local taboos, and terrain memory.
Make a Gathering Check. The GM sets the DC by rarity, concealment, danger, and how much time you spend.
On a success, learn up to three facts:
whether useful materials are present;
approximate rarity;
whether the material is ripe, immature, spoiled, cursed, diseased, guarded, trapped, or spiritually unstable;
the safest obvious method of harvest;
whether the source can survive a careful harvest;
whether the material is compatible with Alchemy, Medicine, Crafting, Beast Taming, Formations, Talismans, or another system;
one obvious environmental danger connected to the harvest.
On a failure, you learn only one incomplete fact. On a failure by 5 or more, the GM may give a misleading impression, reveal the source to a guardian, or cause the source to begin spoiling.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":38467,"source_global_line_start":38448,"source_heading":"Read the Sign","source_local_line_end":107,"source_local_line_start":88,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Multi-Mode","canonical_action_type":"Multi-Mode","factory_combat_bucket":"action","factory_routing":"combat_action_plus_field_examination_variant","range":"60 feet for obvious signs; 1 mile during wilderness survey; special talents may extend this","timing_detail":"Search or Study action, or 10 minutes of field examination"}},{"component_name":"Clean Harvest","exact_source_excerpt":"### Clean Harvest
Action Type: Ritual Procedure
Canonical Action Type: Ritual Procedure
Factory Routing: procedure_ledger_not_combat_action
Timing Detail: 10 minutes for ordinary work; Action only if a talent says so
Requirement: appropriate tools or a justified improvised method
Make a Gathering Check against the source’s Harvest DC.
Success: You harvest one usable Lot or specific ingredient from the source.
Success by 5 or More: Choose one benefit:
harvest one additional Lot if the source has enough material;
create a Pure Lot;
avoid harming the root, nest, vein, corpse, or future site;
learn one additional fact from Read the Sign;
avoid one minor hazard associated with the harvest.
Success by 10 or More: Create a Perfect Lot if the source can support it, or choose two benefits from Success by 5 or More.
Failure by 5 or Less: You harvest a Bruised Lot, or the Lot requires immediate sealing to remain useful.
Failure by More Than 5: The attempted Lot is lost, spoiled, contaminated, scattered, or destroyed.
Natural 1: The attempted Lot is lost and a hazard triggers if one exists. Examples include poisonous spores, biting insects, a guardian beast’s attention, a snapped root, spiritual backlash, corrosive sap, falling stone, bad air, or a broken tool.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":38485,"source_global_line_start":38468,"source_heading":"Clean Harvest","source_local_line_end":125,"source_local_line_start":108,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Ritual Procedure","canonical_action_type":"Ritual Procedure","factory_routing":"procedure_ledger_not_combat_action","requirement":"appropriate tools or a justified improvised method","timing_detail":"10 minutes for ordinary work; Action only if a talent says so"}},{"component_name":"Harvest Lots and Lot Quality","exact_source_excerpt":"### Harvest Lots
A Harvest Lot is a sealed, usable portion of raw material.
Examples include:
a bundle of medicinal herbs;
a spirit fruit;
bark curls from a sacred tree;
sealed dew from moon grass;
a fungus cap harvested at the correct hour;
powdered mineral scraped from a vein;
venom in a waxed ampoule;
beast gall, horn, claw, hide, bone, scale, silk, shell, or blood;
a low-grade beast core;
a jar of spirit soil;
resin, incense wood, or aromatic root;
petals, seeds, pollen, or bitter leaves.
A Lot must come from a source that exists in the scene, downtime, market, sect field, wilderness route, or strategic project. This sphere does not create Lots from nothing.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":38510,"source_global_line_start":38495,"source_heading":"Harvest Lots","source_local_line_end":150,"source_local_line_start":135,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Seal the Essence","exact_source_excerpt":"### Seal the Essence
When you finish a Short Rest or spend 10 minutes with a harvested Lot, you may seal a number of Lots equal to your Proficiency Bonus + Gathering key ability modifier, minimum 1.
Sealed Lots do not spoil under ordinary travel conditions for 7 days.
At CL 5, your sealed Lots remain stable for 30 days.
At CL 10, your sealed Lots remain stable for 1 year if stored properly.
At CL 15, your sealed Lots remain stable indefinitely unless damaged, cursed, stolen, spiritually consumed, or exposed to an incompatible environment.
Sealing does not protect against every supernatural condition. Fire orchids may still require heat. Moon dew may still require darkness. Thunder fruit may still need copper seals. The GM may require special containers for Rare or higher materials.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":38522,"source_global_line_start":38516,"source_heading":"Seal the Essence","source_local_line_end":162,"source_local_line_start":156,"structured_fields":{}}]

[{"component_name":"Centered Breath","exact_source_excerpt":"##### Centered Breath
When you take the Dodge action while unarmed or using only Unarmed Strikes, regain Martial Focus if it is absent.
Once per turn when you hit a creature with an Unarmed Strike while holding Martial Focus, you may move 5 feet without provoking Opportunity Attacks from that creature.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":46781,"source_global_line_start":46779,"source_heading":"Centered Breath","source_local_line_end":164,"source_local_line_start":162,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Iron Fist Training","exact_source_excerpt":"##### Iron Fist Training
When you learn Iron Fist:
gain proficiency in Strength (Athletics), Dexterity (Acrobatics), or Wisdom (Medicine);
if already proficient in all three, choose one Iron Fist talent for which you meet the prerequisites as a bonus talent;
choose Strength or Dexterity as your Iron Fist ability;
gain the Iron Fist Die;
choose one Mortal Realm Form talent for which you meet the prerequisites.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":46776,"source_global_line_start":46770,"source_heading":"Iron Fist Training","source_local_line_end":159,"source_local_line_start":153,"structured_fields":{}}]

[{"component_name":"Magma Glob","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Magma Glob
Classification: Magma Base Attack
Action Type: Action
Factory Combat Bucket: action
Factory Routing: combat_action_row
Range: 60 feet
Cost: None
Launch molten stone, slag, obsidian shards, or volcanic force at one creature or object.
Make a ranged Cultivation Attack.
Hit: The target takes Fire or Bludgeoning damage, chosen when you attack.
Damage by Cultivation Level:
• CL 1-4: 1d10
• CL 5-10: 2d10
• CL 11-16: 3d10
• CL 17-20: 4d10
Once per turn when Magma Glob hits, choose one:
• create one 5-foot patch of Molten Terrain adjacent to the target until the start of your next turn;
• apply Heat Pressure until the start of your next turn;
• push the target 5 feet if it is Large or smaller;
• create one 5-foot patch of Obsidian Terrain adjacent to the target for 1 minute.
Magma Glob is powerful for a base elemental attack because Magma is restricted. It is still not a siege technique by itself.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":53540,"source_global_line_start":53520,"source_heading":"Magma Glob","source_local_line_end":180,"source_local_line_start":160,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Action","cost":"None","factory_combat_bucket":"action","factory_routing":"combat_action_row","range":"60 feet"}},{"component_name":"Magma Initiation","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Magma Initiation
When you learn Magma, choose the form your magma takes most often:
• red-gold lava;
• black slag;
• white-hot furnace stone;
• volcanic glass;
• subterranean pressure cracks;
• ash-veiled lava;
• spiritual molten ore;
• another appropriate expression.
This choice is cosmetic unless a feature says otherwise.
You learn to create small amounts of cultivated molten stone for Magma talents. These expressions are magical and fade when the talent ends unless a feature creates lasting obsidian, stone, or ruin.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":53519,"source_global_line_start":53508,"source_heading":"Magma Initiation","source_local_line_end":159,"source_local_line_start":148,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Shape the Crust","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Shape the Crust
Classification: Magma Utility
Action Type: Bonus Action
Factory Combat Bucket: bonus_action
Factory Routing: combat_action_row
Range: 60 feet
Cost: None
Choose one patch of Molten Terrain or Obsidian Terrain you created within range.
Choose one:
• cool one 5-foot patch of your Molten Terrain into Obsidian Terrain for 1 minute;
• crack one 5-foot patch of your Obsidian Terrain, ending it and dealing Slashing damage equal to your Proficiency Bonus to one creature standing in it, Dexterity save negates;
• move one 5-foot patch of your Molten Terrain up to 5 feet across a continuous surface;
• shape one patch into a low ridge that grants Half Cover until the start of your next turn;
• open a safe stepping line through one patch for one ally until the start of your next turn.
Shape the Crust cannot move terrain through walls, across open air, or into a creature’s space unless a talent says otherwise.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":53555,"source_global_line_start":53541,"source_heading":"Shape the Crust","source_local_line_end":195,"source_local_line_start":181,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Bonus Action","cost":"None","factory_combat_bucket":"bonus_action","factory_routing":"combat_action_row","range":"60 feet"}}]

[{"component_name":"Capering Footwork","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Capering Footwork - Base Opener
Action Type: Bonus Action
Factory Combat Bucket: bonus_action
Factory Routing: combat_action_row
Duration: Until the end of your current turn
Cost: 1 Stamina or 1 CP through an explicit non-Body access feature
Chain Role: Opener
Adopt an unpredictable movement pattern.
Until the end of your turn:
your Speed increases by 10 feet;
you ignore nonmagical difficult terrain;
you may move through the spaces of creatures larger than you, treating hostile spaces as difficult terrain;
opportunity attacks against you have Disadvantage;
gain Advantage on the next Dexterity (Acrobatics) check you make before the end of the turn;
you may create and spend one Monkey Link.
You cannot end this movement in an occupied space.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":56956,"source_global_line_start":56941,"source_heading":"Capering Footwork - Base Opener","source_local_line_end":159,"source_local_line_start":144,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Bonus Action","cost":"1 Stamina or 1 CP through an explicit non-Body access feature","duration":"Until the end of your current turn","factory_combat_bucket":"bonus_action","factory_routing":"combat_action_row"}},{"component_name":"Laughing Staff and Borrowed Weapon","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Laughing Staff and Borrowed Weapon - Base Weapon Tool
You are proficient with quarterstaffs, staffs, and improvised weapons used through Monkey Style.
Your Monkey Style improvised weapons deal 1d6 damage unless their normal damage would be higher.
Examples include stools, bottles, bricks, cooking pots, chains, loose stones, ladders, teapots, fruit, shoes, roof tiles, sleeves weighted with coins, and stolen weapons used incorrectly.
While Capering Footwork is active, hitting with a staff, Unarmed Strike, or improvised weapon may spend your Monkey Link on a base Finish.
Your Monkey Style improvised weapons count as magical while you wield them when a rule distinguishes magical and nonmagical attacks.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":56970,"source_global_line_start":56965,"source_heading":"Laughing Staff and Borrowed Weapon - Base Weapon Tool","source_local_line_end":173,"source_local_line_start":168,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Monkey Grip","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Monkey Grip - Base Link Tool
You cultivate hands, feet, knees, hooked sleeves, staff leverage, tail-like Qi, and impossible balance.
Gain the following benefits:
gain a Climb Speed equal to half your Speed;
gain Advantage on ability checks made to climb, hang, swing, balance, cling, or keep hold of an object;
you may hang from appropriate terrain using hands, feet, hooked equipment, tail-like Qi, or your staff;
while Capering Footwork is active, climbing, hanging, swinging, or changing elevation may create a Monkey Link.
Monkey Grip does not give you extra hands. You still cannot wield more weapons or shields than your body and equipment reasonably allow.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":56964,"source_global_line_start":56957,"source_heading":"Monkey Grip - Base Link Tool","source_local_line_end":167,"source_local_line_start":160,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Monkey Style Training","exact_source_excerpt":"#### Monkey Style Training
When you learn Monkey Style:
gain proficiency in Dexterity (Acrobatics), Dexterity (Sleight of Hand), or Strength (Athletics);
if already proficient in all three, choose one Mortal Realm Monkey Style talent for which you meet the prerequisites as a bonus talent;
gain Capering Footwork;
gain Monkey Grip;
gain Laughing Staff and Borrowed Weapon;
choose one Mortal Realm Monkey Style talent for which you meet the prerequisites.
This means every Monkey Style practitioner can start the caper, create a link through movement or terrain, spend that link on a basic finish, and use staff or prop fighting as a natural part of the chain.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":56940,"source_global_line_start":56932,"source_heading":"Monkey Style Training","source_local_line_end":143,"source_local_line_start":135,"structured_fields":{}}]

[{"component_name":"Martial Focus","exact_source_excerpt":"### Martial Focus
Retribution uses Martial Focus.
you may hold one Martial Focus at a time;
some talents expend Martial Focus;
expending Martial Focus is not an Action;
you cannot expend Martial Focus if you do not currently hold it;
if a talent says you regain Martial Focus, regain it only if it is currently absent.
Martial Focus represents breath, posture, attention, and the moment in which observation becomes certainty.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":68291,"source_global_line_start":68284,"source_heading":"Martial Focus","source_local_line_end":36,"source_local_line_start":29,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Counterstrike","exact_source_excerpt":"### Counterstrike
Action Type: Action
Canonical Action Type: Action
Factory Combat Bucket: action
Timing Detail: Ready Action; see Extra Attack and Multiattack below
Factory Routing: combat_action_row; preserve attack-replacement detail
Duration: Until the start of your next turn
Cost: None
Prepare one melee weapon attack or Unarmed Strike as a Counterstrike.
When you prepare a Counterstrike:
choose one melee weapon attack or Unarmed Strike you could legally make;
choose one perceivable trigger;
choose one Counter talent you know, if any.
When one of your chosen triggers begins, you may take your Reaction to make the prepared attack against the triggering creature.
A Counterstrike is a specific exception to the ordinary Ready Action:
it resolves before the triggering action, movement, or other activity finishes;
the triggering activity continues normally after your Counterstrike unless your attack, a Counter talent, or another rule prevents it;
you must be able to perceive the trigger;
the triggering creature must be a legal target for the prepared attack when your Counterstrike resolves;
you may ignore a trigger and continue waiting for another chosen trigger before the preparation ends.
Hit: Deal the attack’s ordinary damage and add your Proficiency Bonus to one damage roll of the attack.
After a Counterstrike hits, you regain your Reaction if it is currently expended.
This restored Reaction is a specific exception to the ordinary Reaction limit. It may be spent normally, but it does not allow you to make a second Counterstrike during the same round.
You may make only one Counterstrike per round, regardless of how many triggers you chose or how many Reactions you can take.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":68315,"source_global_line_start":68292,"source_heading":"Counterstrike","source_local_line_end":60,"source_local_line_start":37,"structured_fields":{"action_type":"Action","canonical_action_type":"Action","cost":"None","duration":"Until the start of your next turn","factory_combat_bucket":"action","factory_routing":"combat_action_row; preserve attack-replacement detail","timing_detail":"Ready Action; see Extra Attack and Multiattack below"}}]

[{"component_name":"Learning Sect Stewardship","exact_source_excerpt":"### Learning Sect Stewardship
When you learn Sect Stewardship, choose Intelligence, Wisdom, or Charisma as your Sect Stewardship key ability.
Choose Intelligence if your stewardship is based on records, law, administration, scheduling, scripture catalogues, and logistics.
Choose Wisdom if your stewardship is based on reading people, judging duties, teaching juniors, noticing waste, and understanding sect customs.
Choose Charisma if your stewardship is based on face, introductions, mediation, patronage, public speech, and social leverage.
You gain proficiency in one of the following skills or tools:
History;
Insight;
Investigation;
Persuasion;
Religion;
Calligrapher’s Supplies;
Cook’s Utensils;
Herbalism Kit;
a tool appropriate to your sect duty, with GM approval.
If you already have proficiency in the chosen skill or tool, gain a flat +2 bonus to checks with it when the check directly concerns sect life, records, duties, ledgers, halls, missions, etiquette, or institutional logistics.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72629,"source_global_line_start":72614,"source_heading":"Learning Sect Stewardship","source_local_line_end":87,"source_local_line_start":72,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Sect Check","exact_source_excerpt":"### Sect Check
When a Sect Stewardship talent calls for a Sect Check, roll:
d20 + Proficiency Bonus + Sect Stewardship key ability modifier
You may spend one Merit Die before or after rolling the d20 but before the GM declares the result. Roll the Merit Die and add it to the check.
Unless a talent says otherwise, you cannot add more than one Merit Die to the same d20 roll.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72634,"source_global_line_start":72630,"source_heading":"Sect Check","source_local_line_end":92,"source_local_line_start":88,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Standing","exact_source_excerpt":"### Standing
Standing measures how an institution currently recognizes you.
Standing is not cultivation Realm, social class, noble rank, or universal reputation. It is local and contextual.
There are five ordinary Standing grades.
No Standing. You are a stranger, guest, prisoner, petitioner, or unknown worker.
Servant Standing. You may perform basic labor, receive food or shelter, and be ordered around.
Outer Standing. You are trusted for ordinary duties, simple missions, shared facilities, and basic records.
Inner Standing. You are trusted with restricted duties, better instruction, inner facilities, low-grade resources, and sensitive errands.
Core Standing. You are trusted with disciples, stores, restricted archives, important missions, hall authority, or a named courtyard.
Some institutions may also recognize Elder Standing, Guest Elder Standing, Lineage Heir Standing, Clan Standing, Official Standing, or other campaign-specific statuses. These are story positions and are not automatically gained from this sphere.
When you learn Sect Stewardship, choose one institution where you have Servant or Outer Standing, subject to GM approval. If the campaign begins before you have joined any group, you instead gain the Petitioner status and can use the sphere to earn your first recognized place.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72658,"source_global_line_start":72648,"source_heading":"Standing","source_local_line_end":116,"source_local_line_start":106,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Merit Dice","exact_source_excerpt":"### Merit Dice
Sect Stewardship uses Merit Dice.
Your Merit Die begins as a d4.
Your maximum number of Merit Dice is equal to your Proficiency Bonus, minimum 1.
You regain all spent Merit Dice when you finish a Long Rest if you are in good standing with at least one institution and spend at least 10 minutes fulfilling a duty, checking a ledger, reporting to a superior, instructing a junior, cleaning a shared space, or otherwise honoring your role.
If you are away from any institution, you regain one spent Merit Die when you finish a Long Rest by maintaining records, writing reports, preserving tokens, planning duties, or honoring the sect’s rules while traveling.
You can spend Merit Dice only on Sect Stewardship features unless another rule says otherwise.
Merit Dice cannot be converted into gold, trade goods, CXP, pills, talents, items, or political office unless a talent explicitly permits a limited exchange.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72666,"source_global_line_start":72659,"source_heading":"Merit Dice","source_local_line_end":124,"source_local_line_start":117,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Taking a Place","exact_source_excerpt":"### Taking a Place
When you finish a Long Rest, choose one Sect Role that reflects your current duty.
A role is not a class, job, or permanent rank. It is what you are currently known to be doing.
Common roles include:
Field Hand;
Kitchen Worker;
Messenger;
Dormitory Senior;
Archive Runner;
Medicine Hall Assistant;
Mission Board Reader;
Patrol Clerk;
Guest Attendant;
Stable Hand;
Furnace Cleaner;
Spirit Field Steward;
Lecture Scribe;
Dispute Witness;
Storehouse Aide;
Outer Gate Watcher;
Junior Instructor.
When a non-combat ability check directly relates to your chosen role, you may spend one Merit Die and add it to the check.
Examples:
A Field Hand adds Merit to notice spoiled seed, judge irrigation schedules, or organize harvest labor.
A Kitchen Worker adds Merit to feed a dormitory, identify ration theft, or use the kitchen gossip network.
A Lecture Scribe adds Merit to remember an elder’s teaching or copy a clean set of notes.
A Storehouse Aide adds Merit to find a legal substitute supply or detect forged requisitions.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72703,"source_global_line_start":72677,"source_heading":"Taking a Place","source_local_line_end":161,"source_local_line_start":135,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Accepting a Duty","exact_source_excerpt":"### Accepting a Duty
Once per Long Rest, you may declare a Duty.
A Duty is a clear non-combat obligation such as:
tend the lower herb terraces;
copy the new outer gate roster;
carry a sealed letter to the west courtyard;
arrange food for visiting disciples;
inspect the dormitory spirit lamps;
find why medicine stores are short;
escort a group of new outer disciples to orientation;
prepare the Mission Hall notices;
help a farmer settle a sect tithe dispute;
keep records for a gathering expedition.
While pursuing your Duty, you may spend Merit Dice on any non-combat ability check that directly advances it, even if the check is not tied to your chosen Sect Role.
When you complete the Duty, choose one reward with GM approval:
regain one spent Merit Die;
improve your Standing for one scene;
gain a minor favor from someone involved;
learn one useful rumor;
secure food, shelter, or basic supplies for yourself and up to five companions;
gain Advantage on one later Sect Check related to the same hall, person, or problem.
Completing a Duty should create a record, witness, favor, or obligation. The GM may deny the reward if no one knows, cares, or can verify that the duty was performed.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72725,"source_global_line_start":72704,"source_heading":"Accepting a Duty","source_local_line_end":183,"source_local_line_start":162,"structured_fields":{}},{"component_name":"Petitioning the Hall","exact_source_excerpt":"### Petitioning the Hall
As a non-combat Action during a scene, or during downtime, you may spend one Merit Die to petition an appropriate hall, superior, clerk, family, steward, or local authority for ordinary support.
Roll the Merit Die.
On a 1-2, you receive only the minimum help that your Standing demands.
On a 3-4, you receive useful help without extra trouble.
On a 5 or higher, if your Merit Die is large enough to roll that result, the support is unusually timely, discreet, generous, or well-matched.
Common petitions include:
a meal and safe sleeping space;
a clean set of robes or work clothes;
mundane tools for a duty;
permission to enter a public hall;
a basic map of the sect grounds;
an introduction to a clerk, senior, farmer, guard, or apprentice;
access to public notices;
use of a common room, kitchen, stable, outer library desk, or messenger channel;
temporary storage for ordinary goods;
a written receipt or witness statement.
Petitioning cannot obtain rare treasures, secret manuals, combat allies, high-ranking interventions, expensive materials, forbidden access, or anything the institution would not plausibly grant.","match_kind":"source_heading","source_global_line_end":72743,"source_global_line_start":72726,"source_heading":"Petitioning the Hall","source_local_line_end":201,"source_local_line_start":184,"structured_fields":{}}]

